1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,760
A tortoise starts one hundred metres
ahead.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:08,640
Achilles runs ten metres per second; the
tortoise moves at one.

3
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:11,086
First Achilles reaches the tortoise's

4
00:00:11,086 --> 00:00:14,060
starting point, but the tortoise
has moved on.

5
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:17,360
When he reaches that new point, it has
moved again.

6
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:20,400
There are infinitely many of these stages.

7
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,120
How can he ever catch it?

8
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,220
The stages get shorter.

9
00:00:28,220 --> 00:00:32,508
The first takes ten seconds, the next one
second,

10
00:00:32,508 --> 00:00:36,260
then a tenth, then a hundredth, and so on.

11
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:40,629
Their sum approaches ten plus ten ninths,
or one hundred

12
00:00:40,629 --> 00:00:44,600
ninths seconds: about eleven point one one
seconds.

13
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:49,421
The usual constant-speed model gives the
same answer directly:

14
00:00:49,421 --> 00:00:53,780
Achilles closes the hundred-metre gap at
nine metres per second.

15
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:58,100
Infinitely many positive stage times can
have a finite limit.

16
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:02,305
This answers the arithmetic challenge in
that model; the deeper

17
00:01:02,305 --> 00:01:07,120
philosophical questions about motion and
infinity have a longer history.

